  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, specifically preferring the more right-skewed lottery (i.e., the one with higher probability on the lower outcome). In this case, Lottery A is more right-skewed (80% on 0€ vs. 75% for B), so the reason would prefer A. The individual in the report does not mention skewness, concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes, or any explicit comparison of distribution shapes. Instead, the justification centers on choosing 'greater risk' with 'lower probability of winning', which suggests a preference for high-variance, high-reward options. This reasoning is more aligned with risk-seeking behavior or possibly a preference for higher potential gains despite lower odds, rather than a focus on skewness per se. There is no indication that the individual assessed which lottery has more probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. The reasoning in the report emphasizes risk and win probability in a way that may correlate with skewness but does not reflect the rule or information used by the skewness-based reason. Therefore, while both the reason and the individual may end up favoring A, the justification and information used are different.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
